00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
the book of boba fett the new line of  star wars content is by far the most  inconsistent in terms of quality this  show is divided into three parts which  are the upbringing of boba fett's  criminal empire his origin after falling  into the sarlacc pit and her prologue  for the mandalorian season 3. although i  really enjoyed the mandalorian story  development it feels misplaced because  it's taking the spotlight away from boba  fett within his own story when looking  at the run times we only get 66 minutes  of development for boba fett where he  builds his empire in the present  and this isn't including the finale  which is mostly just pure action and  also includes the mandalorian  development so to compare this to how  much screen time we get for the  mandalorian the run time for that is 60  minutes the fact that we don't really  get a solid story for boba fett is so  underwhelming and the story we do get  feels rushed and is riddled with flaws  some rushed aspects include the whole  spice conflict since we never see the  ramifications as to why the spice trade  is ruining the planet besides the pikes  killing some tuscans moss espan the  whole criminal underworld feels very  small and superficial the characters  within the world never break out of  their one-dimensional archetypes and  barely get any screen time like we have  the generic club owner replaceable  random mafia aliens and some forgettable  villains and allies  really the only interesting new  character is a black wookie cursanten  given that he actually has a tangible  backstory and has a cool design unlike  the generic cyberpunk people then the  plot as well is expedited at times an  example being that boba fett wants to  learn to ride a ring core and that it  takes a lot of time to forge a bond with  one to boba fett suddenly riding in at a  rancor during the finale to save the day  without any buildup of him forging that  bond like sure it can happen off screen  but by doing so they are skipping over  that part of the story and the payoff  doesn't feel as earned another example  is the huts giving up so quickly to get  rid of boba fetts they come into the  story since they still believe that  tattooing belongs to them and are  willing to kill boba fett to achieve  that goal and in order to get closer to  that goal they send corsantin to take  out boba which that whole scene is a  complete mess because how did this huge  wookie sneakily infiltrate this palace  does bubba have literally no guards on  watch can anyone stumble in he doesn't  come across as very threatening when his  only muscle some pigs and angry robo  teams besides fenic obviously but  getting back to the assassination  attempt it falls into the action trope  of the antagonist being able to kill the  hero but instead throws them around  because they have plot armor and aren't  allowed to die  once corsantin or chrysanthen i don't  really care at this point is inevitably  defeated the huts are immediately like  oh okay never mind we're just going to  leave now after trying to kill you sorry  we don't want a war with you or the  mayor  like what did you expect were you going  to get control back that easily this  whole conflict evaporates instantly and  makes this story feel even more rushed  getting into some more writing flaws and  weird story directions let's talk about  boba fett the direction that writers  take him is very pg when this is a story  about a criminal underworld in almost  every scene with boba fett he comes  across as a pushover like he almost got  killed by assassins twice and each time  he lets them go and he never comes  across as hostile  even when people are talking to him and  are being disrespectful he doesn't clash  back or show that he should be respected  then another weird story direction slash  edition is the cyberpunk gang i don't  know who the hell at lucasfilm thought  this was a good idea to have these  stupid vespas in the star wars universe  they look absolutely absurd and they  give off the image of power rangers the  whole chase scene with the cyber kids  scooting after the mayor's spoke person  is honestly one of the worst star wars  scenes i've seen in a while it's  hilarious to me that robert rodriguez  probably thinks this is some badass  chase scene when it comes across as  comical then for one more odd story  direction choice we have the writers  bringing back cad bane just to kill him  an episode later now personally i  believe that he isn't permanently dead  and may come back given that they've  been bringing back characters left and  right by giving the excuse of cybernetic  enhancements to save their lives but if  he stays dead then that's an extremely  weird writing choice  one last thing about the writing is that  there are a lot of small writing  mistakes throughout most of the episodes  i'll run through some quickly boba fett  gets jumped by the nightwind assassins  and instead of stabbing him in a spot  without armor they only shock him just  seems like a very convoluted way to kill  someone and shows that boba's plot armor  again as a side note the editing in the  scene is atrocious and you can even see  boba fett fall down twice  for the rooftop chase  fennec dives off the rooftop when  getting attacked and somehow teleports  in front of the assassins running in an  opposite direction dozens of tuscans get  killed but it doesn't seem like it ever  matters because their village is always  populated boba fett being able to  communicate specific instructions and  plans to the tuscans via hand gestures  is absurd the whole cyber kitty chase  scene was irrelevant given that boba  fett has a jet pack the writers change  the rancor into a misunderstood puppy  bubba fit almost kills himself by  putting a ship into the sarlacc pit  without making sure it was dead first  for a contrived tension-inducing moment  during the finale the gang makes a plan  to station people all over mossaspa to  make sure that they don't get surprised  by the pikes but they somehow waltz in  without anyone noticing cad benches up  at the beginning of the finale and then  disappears to then conveniently reappear  again at the end to fight boba fett and  all the action in the finale really  showcases plot armor since the enemy is  deathly allergic to hitting their  targets  even the big battle droids are clearly  missing this massive group of people  running away  i don't think they hit anyone besides  the rancor at point-blank range  so overall to me the story arc of boba  fett overcoming the pike syndicate was  poorly done especially since the pikes  don't become the main antagonist until  the end of episode 3. then for the  origin story it was extremely hit or  miss on one hand i like the arc of bubba  finding a family in the tuscans and  learning that you're stronger as a pact  and then on the other hand there were  long laborious scenes that made his arc  a slog to get through and made it to  where i wish we were back in the present  because we already know boba's endpoint  and there's not many things in between  that time that are overly intriguing  also tuscans only yell in screech so  it's hard to form any attachment to them  for fenix shand i was excited to see the  relationship build up between her and  boba fett but it felt underwhelming  given that one of her big reasons she's  actually working with him is because  she's getting a cut of the success i was  hoping for a more personal and deep  relationship between them but i guess  that's kind of hard when both boba and  fanuc are pretty bland in terms of  character traits for runtime comparison  all of this backstory takes up 92  minutes in the first four episodes which  is significantly more screen time than  what we get in the present so the fact  that we primarily get an origin story  when the show is advertised as boba fett  controlling a criminal underworld is  undeniably disappointing  then for the third surprising piece of  the show we have the mandalorian season  3 and pretty much everything about it is  excellent the writing direction of the  story production design and action  choreography  i love that din jarn was essentially a  noob with the dark saber and ended up  injuring himself with it it doesn't  paint him as a mary sue who instantly  knows how to use a lightsaber after  picking it up like ray or finn he  actually has to learn how to use it  which allows for character growth for  baby yoda i was perfectly satisfied with  how his story ended in season 2 of the  mandalorian where was a heartfelt  goodbye to a traveling companion that  blossomed into a father and son  relationship and by saying goodbye  denjarin was doing what was best for  grogu to me that was a magnificent  ending and by bringing him back i hope  it's not just because they caved into  the baby yoda hype sensation and  actually have a solid future for grogu  and i'm not saying that i disliked his  return i thoroughly enjoyed his scenes  where he trained with luke and meeting  up with din jarn didn't feel contrived  in any way since grogey made that  decision naturally but getting back to  the book of boba fett it's weird how  dave filoni and john favreau chose not  to include all of that into season three  of the mandalorian it kind of comes  across as making the book of boba fett a  required viewing to continue the  mandalorian storyline if you initially  weren't interested in boba fett so yeah  those are the drastic lows and highs of  the show and let me know in the comments  what you thought and thanks for watching  [Music]

